//! member: true
//!
//! std.option — the absence convention over `!T` rows, and the six
//! helpers that name its commonest shapes. Stability tier: core.
//!
//! There is no `?T` and no null (D30): absence is the row tag `none` —
//! `fn find(…) -> int ! {none}` — consumed by `else` (default and
//! `|pat|` forms) and propagated by `?`. Domain-specific absence tags
//! (`eof`, `gone` — `weak.upgrade()` already returns `T ! {gone}` per
//! `[mem.shared.rc.3]`) are legal where the noun earns it; `none` is the
//! default. API-CONVENTIONS §2 is the binding statement; this module is
//! its worked home.
//!
//! **THE SIX LAND HERE (sc06), five sprints after they were designed.**
//! They were blocked from sc01 to sc05 by one grammar fact: a `!`-row
//! parsed only in RETURN position, so no helper could name its own
//! argument (F-0002, then its interpreter half F-0023). lupin 0.1.3
//! landed rows in parameter and `let` positions and 0.1.4 executes them,
//! so every signature this header used to carry as a comment is a body
//! below. Each is thin over `else` by design — a reader learns the
//! language form first, and these are names for the four commonest
//! shapes plus the two that ask a question about absence:
//!
//! - `or(v, default)`  ≡ `v else default`
//! - `to_list(v)`      ≡ 0 or 1 elements
//! - `exists(v)`       ≡ `else |_|` marking
//! - `is_none(v)`      ≡ `else |_|` marking, negated
//! - `expect(v, msg)`  ≡ `v else |_| { … trap … }` — written, executing,
//!   and homed in `std/x/option_expect` (F-0040: a diverging handler
//!   needs a bottom type wolf does not spell)
//! - `flatten(v)`      ≡ collapsing `(T ! {none}) ! {none}` — written,
//!   executing, and homed in `std/x/option_flatten` (F-0039: wolfc's
//!   grammar admits one `!` per type)
//!
//! The two nursery residents are not a hedge: each is a complete body
//! with a test, refused by ONE implementation for a reason with an issue
//! number, and one rejected body poisons a module for every importer — so
//! keeping them on this page would cost the other four their clean static
//! ladder. They graduate back here verbatim, in the pin bump's own
//! commit, when their diagnostics stop firing.
//!
//! **Absence has no literal, which is why nothing here carries a fenced
//! example.** A row VALUE can only be produced by a function that raises:
//! `none` resolves at a raise site and nowhere else, so `option.or(none,
//! 9)` is `unsupported: none does not resolve` and there is no expression
//! an example line could pass to any function on this page (F-0038). §4's
//! rule then applies exactly as written — unrunnable means prose — and
//! the runnable evidence lives in `tests/option/`: six entry files, one
//! per helper, each with a local producer and both a hit and a miss.
//!
//! **The lane story**: these six are generic over `T` and take a row, so
//! lupin runs them, the checked tier answers `unsupported` at the module
//! boundary (F-0015: a raise inside an imported module's item), and the
//! native rung refuses monomorphization. One executing lane is what a
//! generic row helper gets at these pins, and it is the lane that matters
//! here — the bodies are three lines each; what they encode is a
//! convention.
//!
//! Lazy (closure-taking) variants — `or_else` and friends — still wait on
//! c05, and `Option[T]`/`Result[T, E]` as DATA still wait on s16 plus the
//! s15 `try` bridge. The recorded decision stands: std wants exactly one
//! of reified rows or generic data, and now that the row helpers execute,
//! the row half is the one carrying its weight.

/// `v`'s value, or `default` when `v` is absent. The eager-default form:
/// `default` is evaluated whether or not it is needed, so keep it cheap
/// (a lazy `or_else` waits on closures, c05).
///
/// Rows: consumes `none` — the caller receives a `T` and no row.
/// Total: no traps of its own.
///
/// Example (prose — a row value has no literal, F-0038; the runnable form
/// is `tests/option/or_else_default.lu`): with
/// `fn find(x: int) -> int ! {none}`, `option.or(find(5), 9)` is `5` and
/// `option.or(find(0), 9)` is `9`.
pub fn or[T](v: T ! {none}, default: T) -> T {
    v else default
}

// `expect` IS WRITTEN AND IT IS NOT HERE EITHER — `std/x/option_expect`
// holds it, blocked on the language's missing BOTTOM TYPE (F-0040). Its
// handler traps and therefore produces nothing, but wolfc types an
// `else |_| { … }` block by its last expression and `assert(false)` is
// `()`: `E0401: this is (), but the else fallback must produce T`. For a
// MONOMORPHIC helper the workaround is a dummy value after the trap
// (`0`, unreachable); for a generic one there is no `T` to conjure, which
// is exactly what a `never`/`!` type is for. lupin accepts the diverging
// handler and runs it. Same containment reasoning as `flatten` above.

// `flatten` IS WRITTEN AND IT IS NOT HERE — `std/x/option_flatten` holds
// it, and the reason is a grammar divergence rather than a design choice
// (F-0039). Its type is the only one of the six that nests a row inside a
// row (`T ! {none} ! {none}`), and wolfc's grammar admits exactly one `!`
// per type in every position: the nested form is `fail(E0201)` at PARSE
// there, where lupin parses and executes it. A rejected body poisons the
// whole module for every importer, so keeping `flatten` on this page
// would cost the other five their clean static ladder — 5 rows moving
// from `unsupported` (honest refusal, ladder proven) to `fail(E0201)`
// (refused outright). The nursery is where sc01 put the same shape of
// problem, and this is what it is for. The function moves back into this
// module, verbatim, on the pin that makes wolfc's `type` production
// right-recursive.

/// `v` as a list: one element when present, empty when absent. The bridge
/// from absence into the container vocabulary — `for x in
/// option.to_list(v)` is the loop that runs zero times or once.
///
/// Rows: consumes `none`.
/// Total: no traps. The list is allocated in the ambient region (§5).
///
/// Example (prose, F-0038; runnable in
/// `tests/option/to_list_roundtrip.lu`): `option.to_list(find(5)).len` is
/// `1` and `option.to_list(find(0)).len` is `0`.
pub fn to_list[T](v: T ! {none}) -> List[T] {
    var out = List[T]()
    let hit = v else |_| { return out }
    (mut out).push(hit)
    out
}

/// True when `v` is present. This is the question `else` answers by
/// marking, and answering it CONSUMES the value — a caller that wants the
/// value should reach for `or`/`expect` rather than asking first and
/// calling again.
///
/// Rows: consumes `none`.
/// Total: no traps.
///
/// Example (prose, F-0038; runnable in `tests/option/exists_marking.lu`):
/// `option.exists(find(5))` is `true`, `option.exists(find(0))` is
/// `false`.
pub fn exists[T](v: T ! {none}) -> bool {
    let hit = v else |_| { return false }
    true
}

/// True when `v` is absent — `exists`'s complement, shipped because
/// `if option.is_none(v)` reads better than `if !option.exists(v)` at the
/// call sites that ask it that way round. Same consumption caveat.
///
/// Rows: consumes `none`.
/// Total: no traps.
///
/// Example (prose, F-0038; runnable in
/// `tests/option/is_none_marking.lu`): `option.is_none(find(0))` is
/// `true`, `option.is_none(find(5))` is `false`.
pub fn is_none[T](v: T ! {none}) -> bool {
    let hit = v else |_| { return true }
    false
}
